```
So after a fatal SDK error, the message is cleared a moment later. Valid. Fix: preserve the error across the SDK-error-driven stop. Options: add a parameter `adoptSession(nextId, { keepError: true })` or set the error after adoptSession. Minimal: in the SDK error handler, capture message and `setError(message)` AFTER adoptSession. Since adoptSession early-returns when id is unchanged... Also note `adoptSession` returns early if same id — then setError(null) isn't called. But for a stop, id equals current sessionId → early return → error preserved. Hmm! Let me check: sessionIdRef.current is set when session started; on stop, meta.id === current id → `if (sessionIdRef.current === nextId) return;` → early return, so setError(null) is NOT called. So the error is preserved in the fatal case? 

Wait: if the SDK error happens before adoptSession was ever called with the id... The session start sets `adoptSession(started.sessionId)` so sessionIdRef is set. So on stop, same id → early return → error preserved. So this sub-issue may be NOT valid in practice. But the state resets (samples etc.) are also skipped by the early return, which is intentional.

Hmm, but the review also says "a late start response cannot change the stopped phase back to sensing". Let's examine onStart:
```js
const started = await bridge.startSession(timeControl);
adoptSession(started.sessionId);
if (revokedDuringStart.current || !grantedNow.current) { ... stop ... return; }
setPhase('sensing');
```
